// Relativization / partition-safety, `previous`, with THREE keys interleaved.
//
// All prior partition tests use two principals (one "mine", one "foreign").
// This exercises the `previous` anti-join ("nothing of mine strictly between
// the anchor and now") when TWO DISTINCT foreign principals are interspersed
// between alice's own events — the case that distinguishes "exclude every
// foreign key" from a bug that only excludes the nearest foreign key or
// collapses all foreigns into one.
//
// Differential pair for alice's Read at @4:
//   trace_1: alice Login, BOB Transfer, CAROL Transfer, alice Read.
//   trace_2: alice Login,                               alice Read.
// Under key-local semantics both bob and carol are excluded, so alice's
// previous own event is the Login in BOTH traces -> PERMIT. Under global
// semantics -- or a bug that excludes only one foreign key -- carol's
// Transfer @3 is the immediately-preceding event (not a Login) -> deny,
// diverging from trace_2. Both foreign events are non-Login on purpose, so a
// naive global `previous` denies (making the discriminator sharp).

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    previous within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: context.input.user }
};
